WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.220
- Sometimes I'm able
to do a lot of research

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:03.270
before going to a place,

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.700
and sometimes I have to just wing it.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:06.630
Here's what I know.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.978
Every year, this town, Honiton in
south-west England,

00:00:08.978 --> 00:00:12.600
has a tradition where they
throw hot pennies off balconies

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.150
down onto waiting crowds
who try to catch them.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.310
Apparently, this started
more than 700 years ago

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:18.630
as part of the local fair.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.210
Back then, the pennies
would be scalding hot.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.970
And part of the entertainment,
at least for the people throwing,

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.880
was watching poor people
burn themselves

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.010
as they scrambled to
pick up the pennies.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.690
Back then, pennies were
worth a lot more,

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.356
very roughly, it'd be
the equivalent

00:00:32.356 --> 00:00:34.230
of throwing £1 coins
off a balcony,

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:37.500
so perhaps worth minor burns
if you're desperate?

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.380
Seven centuries later,
they do still throw pennies.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:41.910
But I'm assured they're
not scalding,

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.620
just merely warm.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.190
And that is all I know.
My script ends here,

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.950
but I'm hoping I can find out more
from the folks who run it

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.610
and also not get injured
by flying pennies.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:57.660
- This is my first Hot Pennies
as the Mayor of Honiton.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.970
I've been coming to
Hot Pennies all my life.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.490
It's led predominantly by
the town crier.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.730
It's his event, if you like,
and I'm gatecrashing it.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:10.170
- I'm the town crier and
mace bearer of Honiton.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.740
I've held this position for 27 years

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.540
and today, the Hot Pennies Ceremony
will be my 28th.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.540
We toss pennies from balconies, windows

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:24.750
and if you're brave enough

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.510
to scramble for them,
pick them off the floor,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.490
they're all yours to take home.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.620
- People are pretty much
sorting themselves out by height

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.060
so the kids are going to the front.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.350
The adults are coming to the back.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.280
I hope they're going to be okay.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.480
I hope I'm going to be okay!

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.050
- I've been on the committee,
believe it or not, for 46 years,

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.510
and this is my 47th Honiton Hot Pennies.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.280
When Honiton was granted a Royal Charter,

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.530
they held a fair and of course,

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.460
they wanted people to come into
the town to make it a success,

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.050
but quite a lot of those people

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.630
hadn't paid their landlords their rent

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:58.890
so it was decided that they would
give an amnesty during fair week.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:00.780
And whilst the glove was hoisted,

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.670
no man will be arrested.

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.495
- [SHOUTS] No man shall be arrested.

00:02:05.495 --> 00:02:09.030
- [CROWD CHANTS] No man shall be arrested.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.640
- I've only just thought to bring
something to catch the pennies

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.473
and it's far too late.

00:02:12.473 --> 00:02:15.060
Like, there's people with
upside down umbrellas,

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.737
there's kids with buckets,
and I, uh...

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.100
wish me luck.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.530
- Honiton is famous as
one of the staging points

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.420
on the Exeter to London Road,

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:25.710
and there were more pubs

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.350
on Honiton High Street
than most towns have.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.090
There were wealthy people
who lived in the town

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.860
and who stayed in the inns

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:35.790
and they used to take delight
in throwing out hot pennies

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:40.113
and watching the serfs, I suppose,
burn their fingers.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:42.905
- Here we go.

00:02:44.042 --> 00:02:45.179
Bloody hell!

00:02:48.878 --> 00:02:50.178
Oh!

00:02:54.176 --> 00:02:56.732
Augh! Ow.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:03.240
I took my eye off the pennies
for a moment.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.051
- It's 802 years, this year. We think.

00:03:07.051 --> 00:03:12.300
There would've been a few breaks
over the years for World War whatever.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.720
For years, it's run on nothing

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.340
because all the pennies are
collected in jars in the pubs

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.420
and we do throw out

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:24.000
hundreds and hundreds of
pounds' worth of pennies.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.090
- All the pubs would have a large bottle
or a can or a tray

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.070
and people would put
their loose change in it.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.690
Nowadays you have to order the pennies

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:33.510
two weeks in advance from the banks
to even have them.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.030
- Come on!

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:40.050
It's one penny.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.900
It's one penny, but I'm really
proud of the penny.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.144
Can confirm, slightly warm.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.630
- They used to be heated up.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.790
They were literally heated
in the ovens of the pubs.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:57.060
There was a stream running down
one of the sides of the high street.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.970
So there's photographs of kids
in the drains pulling pennies out,

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:00.990
but also cooling them

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:02.880
because they've got burns on their hands.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.600
And I believe there's a lady in Honiton
who, to this day, carries a scar

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.570
from being burnt by a
penny in the mid-'60s.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:14.100
- They used to be proper hot,
I mean, burning, scalding hot,

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.450
and if you caught them on your arm,

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:16.590
I do have a friend from school

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.450
and he claims that he still has a scar

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.490
and he's really proud
of his Hot Pennies scar.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.520
But they'd be scattering on the floor
and it would hurt to pick them up.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.099
- They've got a kid throwing up there

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:29.114
and he's very much
throwing at, and not...

00:04:29.114 --> 00:04:31.143
...augh! and not up.

00:04:32.459 --> 00:04:34.983
- We used to wear gloves,

00:04:34.983 --> 00:04:37.720
hobnail boots, old clothes

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.080
and then when we got back in school,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.750
we'd given 100 yards for skiving off.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.370
- It seems crazy now

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.060
that we used to get
tossed boiling hot coins

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.650
especially when we were
little young children.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.056
But to be honest, that was half the fun!

00:04:52.056 --> 00:04:54.450
- I'm well out of range here.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:58.170
I did not fight hard enough to be
in the crowd with the umbrellas.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:01.516
I'm going to cut my losses
with two pennies

00:05:01.516 --> 00:05:03.660
and one slightly injured camera operator,

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.460
I'm so sorry!

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:07.860
- It was quite a wild event.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.990
Nowadays we have to have
a little bit more careful

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.180
so the pennies are scattered
rather than thrown.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.921
They're warmed in the sun
at best,

00:05:14.921 --> 00:05:16.950
or at worst depending on
where you look at it.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:18.930
We hope we don't have injuries.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:20.250
We're about making certain that

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:23.265
these wonderful old
quaint customs continue.

